[?2004l[?2004h> === TIMESTAMP CORRUPTION BUG - FIXED ===
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> PROBLEM:
[?2004l[?2004h> - 40 out of 200 rows in merged.parquet had NaT (Not a Time) values in the recorded_at column
[?2004l[?2004h> - All NaT values were from sensor_c data
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ROOT CAUSE:
[?2004l[?2004h> - transform.py had a hardcoded datetime format: "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S"
[?2004l[?2004h> - sensor_c.csv used European date format: "DD/MM/YYYY HH:MM:SS" (e.g., 25/03/2024 14:30:00)
[?2004l[?2004h> - Other sensor CSVs used ISO format: "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS" (e.g., 2024-03-01 08:00:00)
[?2004l[?2004h> - The hardcoded format couldn't parse DD/MM/YYYY dates, causing them to become NaT
[?2004l[?2004h> - errors='coerce' silently converted unparseable dates to NaT instead of raising errors
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> SOLUTION:
[?2004l[?2004h> - Modified parse_timestamps() function in transform.py
[?2004l[?2004h> - Changed from: format="%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S"
[?2004l[?2004h> - Changed to: format='mixed'
[?2004l[?2004h> - This allows pandas (v2.0+) to automatically detect and parse multiple date formats
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> VERIFICATION:
[?2004l[?2004h> - Re-ran full pipeline: extract.py -> transform.py -> load.py
[?2004l[?2004h> - Final output: 200 rows, 0 NaT values
[?2004l[?2004h> - All sensor_c timestamps now properly parsed (e.g., 2024-03-25 14:30:00)
[?2004l[?2004h> - Date range: 2024-03-01 08:00:00 to 2024-03-25 17:45:00
[?2004l[?2004h> - All 5 sensors have 40 rows each
